# Oscillations 02 — The period of a pendulum at large amplitude
#
# (Taylor, Classical Mechanics, §4.5 and problem 4.38; Kleppner & Kolenkow ch. 6.)
# A simple pendulum of length L = 1.000 m (g = 9.81 m/s²) is released from rest
# at an angle θ₀.
#
#   (a) The small-angle period T₀ = 2π √(L/g).
#   (b) Energy conservation gives the exact period as an integral with an
#       integrable blow-up at the turning point:
#           T = 4 √(L / 2g) ∫₀^θ₀ dθ / √(cos θ − cos θ₀)
#       and, after the substitution sin(θ/2) = k sin φ with k = sin(θ₀/2),
#           T = 4 √(L/g) K(k),   K(k) = ∫₀^{π/2} dφ / √(1 − k² sin² φ).
#       Evaluate both for θ₀ = 10°, 45°, 90°, 150° and 179°.
#   (c) Compare with the series T ≈ T₀ (1 + θ₀²/16 + 11 θ₀⁴/3072).
#   (d) Solve θ'' = −(g/L) sin θ and measure the period as four times the time
#       of the first zero crossing.

L = 1.000 m
g = 9.81 m/s²
T0 = 2π √(L / g)
print "small-angle period:", T0 to 8 digits

K(k) = ∫ 1 / √(1 - k² sin(φ)²) dφ from 0 to π/2
T_elliptic(θ0) = 4 √(L / g) K(sin(θ0 / 2))
T_energy(θ0) = 4 √(L / (2*g)) ∫ 1 / √(cos(θ) - cos(θ0)) dθ from 0 to θ0
T_series(θ0) = T0 (1 + θ0²/16 + 11 θ0⁴/3072)

for θ0 in [10°, 45°, 90°, 150°, 179°]
    solve θ'' = -(g / L) sin(θ)
      with θ(0) = θ0, θ'(0) = 0 rad/s
      for t from 0 s to 4 T_elliptic(θ0)
    solve θ(t_quarter) = 0 for t_quarter from 0 s to 2 T_elliptic(θ0)
    print "amplitude", θ0 in deg, "| elliptic:", T_elliptic(θ0) to 8 digits, "| energy integral:", T_energy(θ0) to 8 digits, "| ODE:", 4 t_quarter to 8 digits, "| series:", T_series(θ0) to 8 digits
